# Make goldmark faster

## Goal
Reduce the wall-clock time of the workload below while preserving every observable result under the stated output contract.

## Setup
8 CPUs, 16 GiB memory, no network access, and a {{ agent_timeout_seconds }}-second agent timeout ({{ agent_timeout }}). The source you leave in the editable paths when time runs out is what gets evaluated.

## Workload
Render a fixed batch of 10,392 distinct complete Markdown documents, totaling 116,306,743 input bytes, to HTML. Each document is processed once. One GFM parser and HTML renderer are configured before the batch and reused across documents. Each document receives a fresh parse context, syntax tree and output buffer.

The driver constructs the parser with `extension.NewGFMParser()` and the HTML renderer with `extension.NewGFMHTMLRenderer()`. It calls `Parse` on each complete document and then `Render` into that document's output buffer. The pinned API exposes parser and renderer constructors separately.

A second unit, `parse-only`, uses a separate protected driver on the same documents. It configures one parser with auto heading IDs, GFM and footnotes, parses each document and walks its syntax tree without rendering, writing records for headings, links, images, autolinks, fenced code blocks and footnote counts. Both units are timed.

Timing covers the driver process from start to exit, including configuration, file reads, parsing, rendering and writing complete results. Input staging and output comparison happen outside execution. Every document's HTML, record or error output must remain byte-identical to the pinned baseline in both units. Preserve supported Markdown behavior, extensions, options and public APIs beyond the examples; the fixed parser and renderer settings and batch membership must not change.

## What you may edit
In `/app/goldmark`, every existing `.go` file under `ast`, `parser`, `renderer`, `text`, `util` and `extension` is editable except files ending in `*_test.go`. This permits exactly 53 existing production files; do not add or remove files. Limits are 1,000 files and 20,000,000 bytes.

All other paths are protected, including `**/*_test.go`, `**/testdata/**`, module/build files, dependencies, drivers, inputs and manifests. Add no dependencies. Existing attribution must remain intact.

## Build and check
The image contains the pinned source, dependencies and public inputs. These commands rebuild the candidate and check its public behavior; public output checks do not report evaluation timings. The evaluator runs the original upstream tests separately.

```sh
set -eu
OUT=$(mktemp -d)
sh /app/workload/build.sh "$OUT/build"
"$OUT/build/run" > "$OUT/results.tsv"
python3 /app/workload/check_public.py "$OUT/results.tsv"
```

The build requires a fresh output directory; this example creates one each time. The runner emits fingerprints; `check_public.py` compares them with the pinned public baseline.

The upstream `AGENTS.md` requires `make lint`, but `golangci-lint` and `gopls` are not installed. That lint requirement does not apply to this task. Run upstream tests with `cd /app/goldmark && make test`.

## How it is evaluated
A separate clean environment rebuilds your editable source, checks the edit scope, runs the project's tests, and compares outputs on public inputs and hidden inputs of the same kind using the contract above. It then times the original and your version on the hidden inputs, checking every output. Your score is a conservative estimate of the wall-clock speedup on the hidden inputs; when there are several timed units, each counts equally. Total CPU time and peak memory are reported but not scored.

## You may
- Change any code in the editable paths, including algorithms, data structures and internal interfaces.
- Add to the public interface, as long as every existing use keeps working.
- Use all 8 CPUs and 16 GiB, as long as results do not depend on how many CPUs are available.

## Rules
- Preserve all supported behavior, validation, errors and the public interface; compatible additions are allowed.
- Do not modify, remove or weaken existing tests. Changes outside the editable paths are ignored.
- No caching across calls or processes, no input or evaluator recognition, no precomputed results, and no delegation to other libraries or programs.
- Do not change process-wide settings, including GC, allocator, environment, signal handlers, locale, working directory, resource limits or CPU affinity.
- When a public call returns (or the program exits, for command-line workloads), nothing it started may still be running.
- Do not change the workload's fixed settings, flags, inputs, operation order or measurement boundaries. Do not defer required work beyond the measured operation.
- Results must be the same on every run, on any number of CPUs, and across repeated calls in the same process.
- Overlapping or aliased arguments, and short or failed reads and writes, must behave as in the original.
- No memory errors, undefined behavior or crashes, including on valid inputs the workload does not use.
